  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nAs core of the aforementioned mid-level ridging redevelops east \nover the central CONUS with accompanying large scale sinking \nmotion, a warming trend in high temperatures is expected through \nmid-week. Hazardous heat is most likely during this period. Highs \nrise into the 100F-105F range over much of the SE NM plains into \nUpper Trans Pecos and northern and central Permian Basin, \napproaching the 95F- 100F range for higher elevations, and up to \n110F for the Big Bend. Heat advisories may be warranted for the \nBig Bend given heat indices also up to 110F forecast for the Big \nBend. Even in regions not meeting hazardous heat criteria, \neveryone is still encouraged to exercise proper heat safety \nprecautions (drink plenty of water, stay out of direct sunlight \nduring the hottest part of the day, postpone any physical activity\nto early morning or evening if possible, and do not leave pets or\npeople unattended in vehicles). Similar to Monday, increased \nheating of elevated terrain with warmer daytime temperatures allow\nfor low (10% to 20%) shower/storm chances to develop southwest of\nthe Pecos River. Wednesday will be a similar story to Tuesday, \nbut feature the warmest high temperatures of the week, with \nwidespread triple digits in the western and northern lowlands, and\nmid to upper 90s F for higher elevations and southeast Permian \nBasin. Low (10% to 20%) shower/storm chances during the \nafternoon/evening are again expected to develop with heating of \nterrain. However as on Monday and Tuesday, overall storm coverage \nand duration remain uncertain due to weak larger scale forcing for\nstorm organization.\n\nThe second half of the week features a cooling trend and \nincreased shower/storm chances. Mid-level ridging develops off to \nthe east of the area, resulting in increasing south/southwest \nreturn flow that strengthens lee troughing and accompanying near \nsurface lift and moisture convergence for low to medium (25% to \n40%) shower/storm chances from the Presidio Valley into SE NM \nplains and northwest Permian Basin. Increased clouds limiting \ndiurnal heating bring highs Thursday back down into the 95F-100F \nrange for the lowlands. Daily shower/storm chances persist for \nwestern higher terrain through early next weekend as mid-level \ntroughing develops over the Intermountain West and disturbances \naloft within southwest flow ahead of the trough brush the western \nportions of the area. High temperatures decrease further during \nthe end of the week through next weekend with increased clouds and\nstorm coverage, below triple digits for most of the lowlands and \nbelow the 90s F range for higher elevations on Friday and \nSaturday. Continued low to medium (25% to 40%) shower/storm \nchances each afternoon/evening southwest of the Pecos River are \nforecast, with locally higher (45% to 55%) rain chances over the \nMarfa Plateau. At this time, rainfall accumulations of a few \ntenths of at least a few tenths of an inch are apparent in grids \nfor the Marfa Plateau for the latter part of the week, but this \nmay increase in coming days. Lows do not change much from earlier \nin the week despite the more humid and rainy monsoonal pattern.",
